# Guardrails for agentic coding

AI agents write much of the code; humans direct, review, and verify.
These guardrails keep that from spiralling; first applied in
genenetwork4:

=> ../gn4/gn4-api-foundation.gmi GN4 API foundation

1. Tests are the gate. An endpoint does not exist until its Rust test
   AND its mirrored Python test pass. Agents must generate the test
   with (or before) the endpoint - never after.
2. guix build -L . -f guix.scm is the canonical build. If the vendored
   crates (rust-package.scm) drift from Cargo.lock, the build fails
   loudly instead of quietly downloading new code.
3. Spec-first. openapi.json changes land in the same commit as the
   route; the validation test makes drift impossible to merge.
4. One envelope. response.rs is the only way to return data; agents
   cannot invent per-endpoint JSON shapes.
5. Small commits, one endpoint at a time. The proxy -> native pattern
   keeps every change reversible.
6. No new dependencies without a human ok. Each new crate must be
   vendored deliberately (guix import crate), so dependency growth is
   visible in review.
7. Typed everything. serde structs for every payload; no raw stringly
   JSON. The compiler plus the envelope types catch agent mistakes at
   build time - this is exactly why Rust was chosen.

